/* ═══ revamp1178 — stacked-nav mode: ONE page gutter, every page ══════════
   In the layout where the grey identity band drops to its own line under the
   main nav, the three stacked bands each ran on a different left edge, and which
   one was the odd one out changed from page to page (#img1308):

                       home 644   home 1101   topic 644   topic 1101
     band title           16          32          16          32
     tab strip            16          16          16          16
     body content         20          32          16          16

   One value settles all of it. The band's inset IS .topic-banner's own padding —
   16, stepping to 32 when the sidebar docks — so the banner becomes the single
   definition of the page gutter and the other two follow it. That makes the
   gutter a property of the MODE rather than of the page: 16 normally, 32 docked,
   on home and topic alike. The homepage body's 20 and the tab strip's missing
   docked step were the two actual defects; the topic body's flat 16 was the
   third, and it only looked correct because its tab strip was wrong in the same
   direction.

   Deliberately not scoped to .home-search. A per-page override is what produced
   four different answers in the first place. */

/* Body content. */
body.tt-on:not(.nav-docked) #topic-layout {
  padding-left: 16px !important;
  padding-right: 16px !important;
}
body.tt-on.nav-docked #topic-layout {
  padding-left: 32px !important;
  padding-right: 32px !important;
}

/* The tab strip. It was pinned at a flat 16 by the shared
   `body.tt-on .topic-viewtabs` shorthand, which has no docked step at all.
   Longhand, so that shorthand's vertical padding survives.

   The #sub-header half of each pair is not decoration: the homepage rule being
   replaced is written `body.home-search.tt-on #sub-header .home-viewtabs, …`,
   and an id outranks any number of classes — a class-only selector loses here
   however specific it looks.

   The [class][class] repeats are the same: dropping `.home-search` to make this
   universal also dropped a specificity point, and under 640px there is a
   `body.home-search.tt-on #sub-header .home-viewtabs` rule tightening the strip
   to .75rem that then won — small phones alone came back misaligned at 12. */
body.tt-on[class][class] #sub-header .home-viewtabs,
body.tt-on[class][class] #sub-header .topic-viewtabs,
body.tt-on[class][class] .home-viewtabs,
body.tt-on[class][class] .topic-viewtabs {
  padding-left: 16px !important;
  padding-right: 16px !important;
}
body.tt-on.nav-docked[class][class] #sub-header .home-viewtabs,
body.tt-on.nav-docked[class][class] #sub-header .topic-viewtabs,
body.tt-on.nav-docked[class][class] .home-viewtabs,
body.tt-on.nav-docked[class][class] .topic-viewtabs {
  padding-left: 32px !important;
  padding-right: 32px !important;
}
/* The topic strip sits INSIDE the band, so it is already carrying the gutter
   from an ancestor; its -16px pull existed to cancel that and full-bleed the
   strip's rule to the container edge before re-applying the inset as padding.
   That pull has to equal the gutter, so it scales with it — left at -16 against
   a 32px gutter it lands the tabs at 64, twice as far in as its neighbours. The
   home strip needs none of this: it lives in the fixed sub-header, outside the
   band entirely. */
body.nav-docked.tt-on .topic-band .topic-viewtabs {
  margin-left: -32px !important;
  margin-right: -32px !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1171 — AI Briefings in tab mode: one column, and a longer featured
   set (#img1265/1266) ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════

   (1) Tab mode is a narrow single-purpose surface, but both briefing lists kept
   their desktop grids: the featured row auto-fit to 3 columns at ~838px and the
   by-topic groups took the base 2-column rule from 760px up. Three ~250px
   columns of clamped summary text is unreadable and left a ragged half-empty
   last row. One column, on both lists. */
body.tt-on #content .dih .dih-items,
body.tt-on #content .dih .dih-group.dih-group--featured .dih-items {
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) !important;
  column-gap: 0 !important;
}

/* (2) The featured set runs to 15 in tab mode, where there's a whole page to
   fill; the desktop grid still leads with four. Both are in the DOM (see
   FEATURED_LEAD in renderIntelligenceHub) so a resize across the threshold
   needs no re-render — and a display:none card never intersects the observer
   that fills it, so the tail costs nothing until tab mode shows it. */
body:not(.tt-on) #content .dih .dih-item--extra { display: none !important; }

/* ═══ revamp1176 — "Change Page" picker on the non-topic pages ═══════════════
   Same .topic-subnav-picker the topic pages use, so it inherits the trigger,
   the panel, the open/close animation and wireSubnavPicker() wholesale.
   revamp1181: and now the same actions row too — View All Topics / Search
   Custom Topic as pills above the separator with the ✕ at its right, rather
   than two more rows in a list of pages. The --bare variant it used while
   that row held only a ✕ is retired; the class is kept styled in case any
   other surface still renders it. */
.tsp-actions--bare {
  justify-content: flex-end !important;
  border-bottom: 0 !important;
  padding: 0 !important;
  margin-bottom: 6px !important;
}
.topic-bodyhead .tsp-actions--bare,
.is-page-picker .tsp-actions--bare { background: none !important; border-bottom: 0 !important; }
/* The page names are short and fixed — two even columns read as a menu rather
   than a topic grid, at every width the panel opens at. */
.is-page-picker .tsp-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
@media (max-width: 560px) { .is-page-picker .tsp-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }

/* The trigger sits at the right end of the grey ident bar, after whatever
   control that page already carries (Expand all / Include Sports Trends). */
#sub-header .topic-subnav-inner,
#sub-header .home-ident-row { align-items: center; }
#sub-header .is-page-picker { margin-left: auto; flex: none; }
#sub-header .topic-subnav-inner > .pagenav-toggle,
#sub-header .topic-subnav-inner > .trend-sports-toggle { margin-left: auto; }
#sub-header .topic-subnav-inner > .pagenav-toggle + .is-page-picker,
#sub-header .topic-subnav-inner > .trend-sports-toggle + .is-page-picker { margin-left: 14px; }

/* ═══ revamp1177 — Home tab mode: the first story sat on the tab strip ═══════
   4px of top padding (revamp1140) put the headline hard against the strip's
   rule the moment the page loaded (#img1316). Give it the same air the rest of
   the list keeps between stories. */
body.home-search.tt-on .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .news-list > .news-card:first-child {
  padding-top: 12px !important;
}
body.home-search.tt-on .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .news-list > .news-card:first-child .news-card-link {
  padding-top: 0 !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1179 — stacked-nav mode, part two: the gutter reaches the pages the
   nav-dropdown shell renders, and ONE seam under the sub-header ═══════════════

   revamp1178 made the gutter a property of the MODE (16, stepping to 32 when the
   sidebar docks) and applied it at #topic-layout. But only Home and the topic
   pages HAVE a #topic-layout: Topics, Trending, AI Briefings, Prompts and Search
   render through the nav-dropdown shell, which carries its gutter on #content
   instead — a flat 32, plus the shell's own 2px scroll padding, so those lists
   ran at 34 against a band title at 16. That single number is every "not wide
   enough / not full width" report on those five pages.

   The vertical seam was the same story told four ways. Measured from the
   sub-header's bottom edge to the first ink:

     home news feed    -3   (the hero-seam pull, below)
     topics             18
     trending            8
     ai briefings       49

   The rule for the mode: content sits ON the sub-header's bottom edge, and each
   surface's own first-row padding supplies the air — which lands every page at
   ~18px of ink offset, the value the topic list already had and the one nobody
   has complained about. The 22px reserve that used to sit under the bar is what
   made the briefings page read as detached; the home feed's hero-seam pull is
   what made it read as jammed. */

/* ── The gutter ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   :not(:has(> #topic-layout)) picks out exactly the shell-rendered pages —
   Home and the topic pages already get theirs from revamp1178 and would
   otherwise double it to 32/64. */
html body.tt-on:not(.nav-docked) #content:not(:has(> #topic-layout)) {
  padding-left: 16px !important;
  padding-right: 16px !important;
}
html body.tt-on.nav-docked #content:not(:has(> #topic-layout)) {
  padding-left: 32px !important;
  padding-right: 32px !important;
}
/* The shell's 2px inline padding is a scrollbar gutter it needs as a DROPDOWN.
   As a page it has no scroller of its own, and it was landing the content at 18
   (or 34 docked) — 2px off its own band title, which is exactly the kind of
   near-miss that reads as broken without being nameable. */
body.tt-on #content .ndp .aii-nav-dd-inner,
body.tt-on #content .ndp .aii-nav-dd-scroll {
  padding-left: 0 !important;
  padding-right: 0 !important;
}

/* ── The seam ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Three [class] repeats: revamp1171 wrote this reserve with one and revamp1175
   with two (to sit the Topics list on the bar). This restates it for every
   pagenav page at once, so it has to outrank both. */
html body.tt-on.pagenav-mode.sticky-always.has-subnav[class][class][class] #content {
  padding-top: calc(var(--nav-h) + var(--subnav-height, 60px)) !important;
}
/* Home. The -0.7rem pull (revamp1173) closes the seam under the blue hero's
   rule — a WIDE-layout concern. In tab mode there is no hero, the tab strip is
   the page's top edge, and the pull dragged the first story 11px up BEHIND it
   (#img1317): its headline sat 9px under the strip. */
body.home-search.app-mode.tt-on .topic-layout.home-grid > .home-sections {
  margin-top: 0 !important;
}
/* Trending. Its -14px caption pull existed only to cancel the 22 above it
   (revamp1175); with the 22 gone it would drag the caption under the bar.
   11px above the caption, which is what the homepage Trending tab uses. */
body.tt-on .ndp.aii-nav-dd-trending .aii-nav-dd-subbar {
  margin-top: 0 !important;
  padding-top: 11px !important;
}
/* AI Briefings. 14px of container padding (revamp1169) on top of the 22 put the
   first briefing 49px off the strip — the "too far from the tabular nav" report.
   The cards carry 13px of their own, so 6 here lands them at the same ~19. */
body.tt-on #content .dih .dih-group--featured .dih-items {
  padding-top: 6px !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1179 — the homepage news feed IS the topic news feed ═════════════

   Three separate reports, one cause: the feed's grid rules are all written
   `.topic-band #section-newsfeed …`, and the homepage feed lives in .home-v2,
   not in a topic band. So it never picked up the two-column grid (it stayed a
   flex column at every width, while a topic page with the SAME 1100px column
   goes to two), and its head and first card kept a set of homepage-only values
   that drifted from the topic page's. Match the topic page on all three. */

/* (1) Two columns, on the same threshold the topic feed uses — tnews-1col is
   already computed from content width (updateTopicViewMode), so both pages flip
   at the same moment. */
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .news-list {
  display: grid !important;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)) !important;
  align-items: stretch !important;
  gap: 14px !important;
}
body:not(.tt-on).home-search.tnews-1col .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .news-list {
  grid-template-columns: 1fr !important;
}

/* (2) The first story is a card like every other one. Three separate rules zero
   its top border — a hangover from when the feed was a list of divider-rows and
   the head's rule stood in for the first row's top edge. As a bordered card it
   just reads as an open box (#img1318). */
body.home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .news-list > .news-card:first-child,
body.home-search .home-v2 .news-list > .news-card:first-child {
  border-top: 1px solid #e8edf5 !important;
  padding-top: 14px !important;
  margin-top: 0 !important;
}

/* (3) The head. revamp1174 pinned Home's two column heads to a 48px box with
   14px under the title and bottom-aligned contents; the topic page (revamp1175)
   pins its two to 44 with 12, centred. Same problem, two answers — and Home's
   is the one that reads cramped (#img1319). Take the topic page's. Both Home
   heads move together, which is the constraint revamp1174 existed to protect. */
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .newsfeed-head,
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 .newsfeed-head,
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 .home-featbriefs .hb-head {
  display: flex !important;
  align-items: center !important;
  box-sizing: border-box !important;
  height: auto !important;
  min-height: 44px !important;
  padding: 0 0 12px !important;
  margin: 0 0 14px !important;
  border-bottom: 2px solid #e0e5ee !important;
}
/* The rule belongs to the head box now, so the row inside it carries none. */
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .newsfeed-headrow,
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 .newsfeed-headrow {
  display: flex !important;
  align-items: center !important;
  width: 100% !important;
  height: auto !important;
  min-height: 0 !important;
  padding: 0 !important;
  margin: 0 !important;
  border-bottom: 0 !important;
}
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 .newsfeed-title,
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 .newsfeed-headrow h2 {
  font-weight: 800 !important;
}
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .news-list,
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 .news-list {
  margin-top: 12px !important;
}
/* revamp1175 hid the head's rule while the first story is open by targeting
   .newsfeed-headrow, which no longer carries it. Follow the rule to its box. */
body.home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed:has(.news-list > .news-card:first-child.news-card--open) .newsfeed-head {
  border-bottom-color: transparent !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1179 — the AI Prompts rows get the topic list's row furniture ════

   The prompt accordions' icon chip is a 20px ground behind a 19px glyph, so the
   grey barely shows and reads as an icon with a smudge rather than the chip
   every other list on the site uses. The topic list's row is the reference:
   a 26px ground at radius 9 behind a 16px glyph, a 15.5px/600 label, and a grey
   hover band across the whole row. Desktop and tab mode alike (#img1320-1323). */
.aii-fi-acc-ic[class],
body.tt-on .aii-fi-acc-ic[class] {
  width: 26px !important;
