}
body.tt-on #sub-header.pagenav [data-trend-sports-nav] { display: none !important; }
body.tt-on .ndp.aii-nav-dd-trending .aii-nav-dd-subbar .tlm-controlbar-inner {
  justify-content: space-between !important;
  flex-wrap: wrap !important;
  gap: 8px 14px !important;
}
/* Two contingencies, in order: drop "Trends" from the label first (the switch
   still says what it does), and only then let the row wrap to a second line. */
@media (max-width: 560px) {
  body.tt-on .ndp.aii-nav-dd-trending .trend-sports-toggle .tst-tail { display: none !important; }
}

/* Topics — the switch stays in the All Topics head at every width (revamp1205
   restored the heads in tab mode); only the band's copy goes, the band being
   the crowded one there. */
body.tt-on #sub-header.pagenav [data-topics-expand] { display: none !important; }

/* ═══ revamp1179 — AI Briefings: a briefing REPLACES its preview card ════════

   Opening a briefing on the directory page left the preview card sitting above
   it, still showing the same summary the briefing opens with, and dropped a
   white bordered panel underneath — so one briefing read as two cards, the
   second one repeating the first (#img1326/1327). The card and the briefing are
   the same object in two states: hide the card while its briefing is open, and
   dress the open panel as the soft-grey briefing card the homepage uses.

   Directory only. On a topic page's AI Briefing tab the briefing is the whole
   surface rather than one row of a list, and its white treatment there is the
   right one — a different UX, deliberately. */
body #content .dih .dih-group .dih-item.is-openitem {
  display: none !important;
}
body #content .dih .dih-brief--inline {
  background: #f4f6fa !important;
  border: 1px solid #e2e7f0 !important;
  box-shadow: none !important;
  border-radius: 14px !important;
  margin: 0 0 14px !important;
  padding: 0.9rem 1rem 1rem !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1179b — two follow-ups measured on the deployed page ═════════════ */

/* (1) The pagenav tab strip (Topics / Trending / AI Briefings / Prompts) is the
   one band still on its own edge: revamp1178 gave the home and topic strips the
   mode's 16/32, but those pages' strips are .home-viewtabs / .topic-viewtabs —
   this one is .prompts-subnav-tabs, and it kept a flat 12. */
body.tt-on #sub-header.pagenav .prompts-subnav-tabs[class] {
  padding-left: 16px !important;
  padding-right: 16px !important;
}
body.tt-on.nav-docked #sub-header.pagenav .prompts-subnav-tabs[class] {
  padding-left: 32px !important;
  padding-right: 32px !important;
}

/* (2) Home's two column heads: min-height alone let the AI Briefing rail head
   grow to 45.8 against the feed head's 44, so their rules landed 1.8px apart —
   the exact failure revamp1174 pinned a fixed box to prevent. Pin it again, at
   the topic page's 44. */
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .newsfeed-head,
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 .newsfeed-head,
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 .home-featbriefs .hb-head {
  height: 44px !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1180 — six follow-ups ════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* (1) The docked gutter reaches the identity band. revamp1178 made the gutter
   16 / 32-when-docked and revamp1179 carried it to the shell pages' content and
   tab strip — but the pagenav band's own inner keeps a flat 16, so docked, the
   page title alone sat 16px in while its tab strip and list sat at 32 (#img1328
   /1329). That mismatch is what still reads as "not full width": the content
   looks inset against a title that isn't. */
body.tt-on.nav-docked #sub-header.pagenav .topic-subnav-inner[class] {
  padding-left: 32px !important;
  padding-right: 32px !important;
}

/* (2) The Prompts page starts at the bar like every other tab-mode page. Its
   shell adds four separate top paddings between the tab strip and the first row
   — 8 on .aii-nav-dd-inner, 2 on .aii-nav-dd-scroll, 4 on .prompts-dd, 8 on
   .prompts-home--v2 — which put the first accordion ~40px of ink below the
   strip where Topics' first row sits at ~18 (#img1330/1331). */
body.tt-on #content .ndp .aii-nav-dd-inner,
body.tt-on #content .ndp .aii-nav-dd-scroll,
body.tt-on #content .ndp .prompts-dd,
body.tt-on #content .ndp .prompts-home--v2 {
  padding-top: 0 !important;
}

/* (3) A story with no summary keeps its buttons on the left. `:only-child →
   margin-left: auto` (revamp740) right-aligned them so the row wouldn't look
   empty, but it makes those two cards break the column's left edge that every
   other card holds (#img1332/1333). */
#section-newsfeed .news-card-body > .news-card-actions:only-child,
.news-card-body > .news-card-actions:only-child {
  margin-left: 0 !important;
  justify-content: flex-start !important;
}

/* (4) In tab mode the sub-header's own bottom rule IS the first story's top
   edge, so the first card must not draw one of its own — revamp1179 restored
   that border for the wide layout, where the cards are bordered boxes and the
   first one read as an open box, and it followed into tab mode where the cards
   are full-bleed rows (#img1334/1335). */
@media (max-width: 899.98px) {
  body.tt-on.home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .news-list > .news-card:first-child,
  body.tt-on.home-search .home-v2 .news-list > .news-card:first-child,
  body.tt-on .topic-band .topic-home #section-newsfeed .news-list > .news-card:first-child {
    border-top: 0 !important;
  }
}
/* …but 880 and up the feed is bordered CARDS even in tab mode (revamp1181),
   and a card without its top edge is the open box revamp1179 closed. The rule
   above is about ROWS, so it belongs to the width where rows exist. */
@media (min-width: 900px) {
  body.tt-on.home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .news-list > .news-card:first-child[class],
  body.tt-on.home-search .home-v2 .news-list > .news-card:first-child[class] {
    border-top: 1px solid #e8edf5 !important;
  }
}

/* (5) The tab strip carries a little more height on every page. The tabs stay
   vertically centred in it, and nothing below moves of its own accord: every
   #content reserve is computed from --subnav-height, which is measured from the
   real sub-header, so the extra height pushes the page down rather than sliding
   under it. */
body.tt-on #sub-header.pagenav .prompts-subnav-tabs[class],
body.tt-on[class][class] #sub-header .home-viewtabs,
body.tt-on[class][class] #sub-header .topic-viewtabs,
body.tt-on[class][class] .home-viewtabs,
body.tt-on[class][class] .topic-viewtabs {
  min-height: 56px !important;
  align-items: center !important;
}

/* (6) Search and Topics in the mobile header. The row carries a 16px flex gap
   of its own, which is what reads as too much between two adjacent pills —
   adding to it (revamp1180: +10, then +4) only ever made it worse. Pull it
   back instead: 8px between the two, half what the row gives everything else,
   because they are one control group rather than two separate items. */
#site-header .nav-searchbar + .navbtn--topics-right,
.sticky-hero-inner .nav-searchbar + .navbtn--topics-right {
  margin-left: -8px !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1180 — Topics is a nav LINK first, a button second ═══════════════

   Topics sat beside Search as a permanent action while Home / AI Briefings /
   Trending / Prompts were links — so the site's second-most-used destination
   was the one thing not in the row of destinations. It is now the second link,
   after Home, and the button beside Search is what it degrades into when the
   row runs out of width (fitMainNav sheds it first, before Home). Exactly one
   of the two is ever on screen; below the mobile breakpoint the links row is
   hidden entirely and the button is the only way in, which is why the inline
   copy is opt-IN (.nav-topics-inline) rather than opt-out. */
.navbtn--topics-inline { display: none !important; }
.sticky-hero-inner.nav-topics-inline .navbtn--topics-inline { display: inline-flex !important; }
.sticky-hero-inner.nav-topics-inline .navbtn--topics-right { display: none !important; }

/* ═══ revamp1180b — the topic strip's reserve follows its new height ═════════

   On topic pages the tab strip is a SEPARATE fixed element that --subnav-height
   (the identity band alone) does not measure, so its height is hard-coded into
   two #content reserves as "+52px". Growing the strip to 56 without moving
   those left the first story 4px behind it. One variable now, so the next
   change to the strip's height only has to be made in one place. */
:root { --tt-tabstrip-h: 56px; }
html body.tt-on.app-mode.has-subnav:not(.home-search):has(.topic-home:not(.tview-brief):not(.tview-tools)) #content,
html body.tt-on.app-mode.has-subnav:not(.home-search):has(.topic-home) #content {
  padding-top: calc(var(--nav-h) + var(--subnav-height, 66px) + var(--tt-tabstrip-h)) !important;
}

/* The Prompts shell keeps a 2px inline padding of its own on .prompts-dd, which
   put its rows 2px inside the gutter its own band and tab strip run on. */
body.tt-on #content .ndp .prompts-dd {
  padding-left: 0 !important;
  padding-right: 0 !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1181 — six fixes ═════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* (1) The "Change Page" trigger was an unstyled browser button on the homepage:
   the `.tsp-btn` reset (border/background/padding off) lives inside a
   narrow-width media block, and the HOME band is the one place that renders the
   picker outside it — so at wider widths it fell back to the UA's 2px outset
   grey chrome (#img1337/1338). The reset belongs to the component, not to a
   width. */
.tsp-btn[class] {
  display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: flex-start;
  gap: 0.45rem; padding: 0.4rem 0.15rem;
  border: none; background: none; box-shadow: none; cursor: pointer;
  text-align: left; font-family: var(--font-family);
  -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
}
.tsp-btn-browse[class] { gap: 0.3rem; padding: 0.3rem 0; width: auto; }

/* (2) The homepage feed keeps its cards for exactly as long as a topic feed
   does. Topic pages switch cards→rows at a VIEWPORT of 880px (revamp765/803);
   Home was switching on `:not(.tt-on)`, which is CONTENT width — so with the
   sidebar docked at 1160 the topic feed showed cards while Home, on the same
   screen, showed flat rows (#img1339). Same trigger for both. */
@media (min-width: 900px) {
  body.tt-on.home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .news-list {
    gap: 14px !important;
    padding: 4px 0 6px !important;
  }
  body.tt-on.home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .news-card {
    display: flex !important; flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch;
    min-width: 0; position: relative; text-align: left;
    border: 1px solid #e8edf5 !important; border-radius: 12px !important;
    background: #fff !important;
    padding: 14px 16px 12px !important; margin: 0 !important;
  }
  body.tt-on.home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .news-card::after { display: none !important; }
  body.tt-on.home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .news-list > .news-card:first-child {
    padding-top: 14px !important;
  }

  /* (3) …and in card mode the first story stops riding the tab strip. Flush is
     right for a row — the strip's bottom rule reads as the row's top edge — but
     a bordered card glued to the strip reads as caught under it (#img1340).
     Rows (below 880) keep the flush seam. */
  /* Specificity, not preference: the rule this replaces is
     `body.tt-on .topic-home:not(.tview-brief):not(.tview-tools) #section-newsfeed
     .news-list` — five classes, because each :not() counts as one. */
  html body.tt-on[class][class] #section-newsfeed .news-list[class][class] { margin-top: 12px !important; }
}

/* (4) The homepage hero: the title starts higher, and the topic chips get real
   room above the rule that closes the band instead of nearly touching it. */
body.home-search .home-hero {
  padding-top: 4px !important;
  padding-bottom: 18px !important;
}

/* (5) The sidebar's five links were two different heights — 47.5 for Home /
   Topic Pages / AI Briefings, 43 for Trending / Prompts. Same padding, same
   icons: the first three are <a>s, which inherit the document's 1.6 line-height,
   while the other two are <button>s, which reset it to `normal`. Pin the label's
   line-height so the element type stops deciding the row height (#img1341). */
.navmenu-quicklink .navmenu-cta-label,
.navmenu-cta .navmenu-cta-label { line-height: 1.2 !important; }

/* ═══ revamp1181b — the homepage title starts where the page does (docked) ═══

   With the sidebar docked, #site-header is translated off-screen (measured at
   top: -56, opacity 0) — but Home still reserves a full var(--nav-h) of
   padding above its hero, so the title sat ~70px down a band with nothing in
   it. revamp1158 already zeroes that reserve for every OTHER page when docked
   and skipped Home only because the hero paints blue behind the padding, which
   hides the gap without closing it. 16px: enough that the title isn't jammed
   under the sidebar's own header, ~40px less than it was. */
html body.nav-docked.home-search.app-mode:not(.tt-on)[class][class] #content,
html body.nav-docked.home-search.app-mode.sticky-always:not(.tt-on)[class][class] #content,
html body.nav-docked.home-search.app-mode.sticky-always.has-subnav:not(.tt-on)[class][class] #content {
  padding-top: 16px !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1181c — "Today's News" sits on its rule the way "News Feed" does ══

   Both heads are a 44px box with 12px of padding under the content, but the two
   titles measure differently: the topic page's .newsfeed-title is a 27.3px line
   box, Home's is 14.2 (its flex child collapses to the glyph box). Centred, that
   difference lands entirely under the title — 12px of padding plus ~15px of
   leftover box, so the rule sits ~30px below the text instead of 12 (#img1342).

   Bottom-align the content instead of centring it: the gap to the rule is then
   the padding and nothing else, whatever the title's box happens to measure.
   Both Home heads keep the same 44px box, so their two rules still land on one
   line — the constraint revamp1174 exists to protect. */
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .newsfeed-head,
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 .newsfeed-head,
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 .home-featbriefs .hb-head {
  align-items: flex-end !important;
}
/* The feed head is a flex COLUMN (the rail head is a row), so on it the axis
   that bottom-aligns is justify-content — align-items alone left the title
   pinned to the top of the box, which is the 30px gap itself. */
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .newsfeed-head,
body:not(.tt-on).home-search .home-v2 .newsfeed-head {
  justify-content: flex-end !important;
  align-items: stretch !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1181f — home, tab mode: the grid's row gap has nothing to space ══
   #topic-layout is a grid whose first two children (the blue hero and the
   body-tabs strip) are display:none in tab mode, leaving one item and an empty
   0px row — but the 25.6px row-gap is still drawn above it, so the first story
   sat ~44px under the tab strip where a card wants ~18. */
body.tt-on.home-search #topic-layout,
body.tt-on.home-search .topic-layout.home-grid {
  row-gap: 0 !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1181g — "Today's News" IS the topic pages' "News Feed" title ═════

   Every previous pass at this styled `.newsfeed-title`, but the element that
   actually renders is the `.newsfeed-title-main` span inside it — the topic CSS
   says so in as many words at line ~18484 ("sizing only the outer
   .newsfeed-title leaves it at its own size"). So Home kept a 19.84px/750 span
   inside a title I had set to 800, against the topic page's 24.8px/800: five
   pixels smaller and a weight lighter, which is exactly what the two
   screenshots show (#img1343/1344).

   This is the topic page's own declaration block (styles.css ~14375, plus the
   800 from .newsfeed-head--topic), restated for Home against the same span —
   same family, same rem sizes, same tracking, same colour, so the two heads
   scale together from here on instead of drifting apart again. */
body.home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .newsfeed-title[class],
body.home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .newsfeed-title-main[class],
body.home-search .home-v2 .newsfeed-title[class],
body.home-search .home-v2 .newsfeed-title-main[class] {
  font-family: var(--font-display) !important;
  font-size: 1.55rem !important;
  line-height: 1.1 !important;
  font-weight: 800 !important;
  letter-spacing: -0.022em !important;
  color: #14213d !important;
  border-bottom: none !important;
  padding: 0 !important;
  margin: 0 !important;
  min-height: 0 !important;
  background: none !important;
  transform: none !important;
}
/* …including the step the topic head takes below 900px. */
@media (max-width: 899.98px) {
  body.home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .newsfeed-title[class],
  body.home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .newsfeed-title-main[class],
  body.home-search .home-v2 .newsfeed-title[class],
  body.home-search .home-v2 .newsfeed-title-main[class] {
    font-size: 1.44rem !important;
  }
}

/* ═══ revamp1183 — the open briefing's close control joins its masthead ══════

   The panel opened with a "Close Briefing" link, a ✕ and a rule of their own
   above the briefing's topic pill / date line — a header bar for a panel that
   already has a header. The homepage briefing card does this properly: one ✕,
   top-right, on the pill's line (#img1345/1346). Same here. The ✕ is the whole
   control now; the foot's "Close Briefing" button (revamp1164) still closes it
   from the bottom of a long read. */
#content .dih .dih-brief--inline { position: relative !important; }
#content .dih .dih-brief--inline .dih-brief-bar {
  position: absolute !important;
  top: 0.95rem !important;
  right: 1rem !important;
  z-index: 4 !important;
  margin: 0 !important;
  padding: 0 !important;
  border: 0 !important;
}
#content .dih .dih-brief--inline .dih-brief-bar .dih-brief-back,
#content .dih .dih-brief--inline .dih-brief-bar .fb-closelink { display: none !important; }
/* The rule that sat under that bar belonged to it. */
#content .dih .dih-brief--inline .dih-brief-title {
  display: none !important;
}

/* In one column the card above an open briefing was still drawing its bottom
   hairline, so a line poked out over the panel's rounded top edge. Featured
   only — the by-topic lists open their briefing under a row that should keep
   its separator. */
#content .dih .dih-group--featured .dih-items > .dih-item:has(+ .dih-brief--inline) {
  border-bottom-color: transparent !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1185 — the topic hero meets the nav when the sidebar is collapsed ═

   #content's reserve is written `calc(var(--nav-h) + 1.35rem)` — the nav's
   height plus a gap, from the era when the page under it was white. The topic
   hero paints a blue band from the top of that content box, so those 21.6px
   show as a white stripe between the nav and the band (#img1347/1348). Docked,
   revamp1158 already zeroes the whole reserve and the band sits flush; this is
   the same fix for the collapsed-sidebar case, where the nav is real and only
   the gap under it has to go. */
html body.app-mode:not(.nav-docked):not(.tt-on):not(.home-search).has-subnav[class][class] #content,
html body.app-mode:not(.nav-docked):not(.tt-on):not(.home-search).sticky-always[class][class] #content,
html body.app-mode:not(.nav-docked):not(.tt-on):not(.home-search).sticky-always.has-subnav[class][class] #content {
  padding-top: var(--nav-h) !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1186 — one briefing masthead, on every surface it appears ════════

   The topic pill → date · time → AI-generated label lockup renders in five
   places (the hub's open briefing, the topic sidebar card open and closed, the
   topic page's AI Briefing tab, the homepage rail), and each had drifted on the
   same four details: the separator dot sat nearer the date than the time, the
   three lines were packed tighter than they read, the AI label had no shape of
   its own (so its hover painted a square), and the section rules under "The Big
   Picture" / "What Matters Today" were a 1px hairline against 17.6px display
   type. Fixed once on the shared components (#img1349-1356). */

/* (1) The dot sits between date and time, not beside the date. Its own
   margin-right (9) has to equal the row's column-gap (7) or it reads off-centre
   — they are the two halves of the same gap. */
.di-mast-when { column-gap: 10px !important; }
.di-mast-date + .di-mast-time::before,
.di-mast-topic ~ .di-mast-time::before,
.di-mast-date ~ .di-mast-time::before { margin-right: 10px !important; }

/* (2) Air between the three lines: the pill, the date line, the label. */
.di-mast { gap: 10px !important; }
.di-page .di-body,
.tdi-card--hero2.is-open .di-page .di-body { gap: 0.7rem !important; }

/* (3) The AI-generated label is a pill wherever it appears — the shape the
   collapsed sidebar card already gives it — so its hover has something to
   round. A touch darker than the card it sits on, and darker again on hover. */
/* [class][class][class]: the tab-mode surface states this at
   `body.tt-on .tview-brief .di-prov2` (three classes) with a translucent ground
   and no border, and .how-aigen states a fourth. This has to outrank both or
   the label keeps a different shape on the one surface where it is largest. */
body.tt-on .tview-brief .di-prov2[class][class][class],
body.tt-on .tview-brief [data-di-host] .di-prov2[class][class][class],
.tdi-cardprov[class][class][class],
.di-prov2[class][class][class] {
  align-self: flex-start;
  padding: 5px 11px !important;
  border-radius: 999px !important;
  background: #e9edf6 !important;
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, #2f4d9e 10%, transparent) !important;
}
@media (hover: hover) {
  body.tt-on .tview-brief .di-prov2[class][class][class]:hover,
  .tdi-cardprov[class][class][class]:hover,
  .di-prov2[class][class][class]:hover { background: #dfe6f3 !important; border-color: color-mix(in srgb, #2f4d9e 18%, transparent) !important; }
}

/* (4) The section rules carry the weight of the type above them. */
.di-sectitle[class] {
  border-bottom-width: 2px !important;
  border-bottom-color: #dfe5ef !important;
  padding-bottom: 9px !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1187 — five fixes ════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* (1) The blue band reaches the screen edge on wide displays. Its bleed is a
   fixed `left/right: -48px` on the hero's ::before, which was enough when the
   page filled the window; past #content's 1760px cap the gutters grow past 48
   and the band stops short with white either side of it (#img1357). Span the
   viewport instead, and clip the overshoot at the app so it can never become a
   horizontal scrollbar. The docked sidebar paints over its own side. */
#app { overflow-x: clip; }
.home-hero::before,
body:not(.tt-on):has(.topic-home) .topic-bodyhead::before {
  left: 50% !important;
  right: auto !important;
  width: 100vw !important;
  margin-left: -50vw !important;
}

/* (2) An open story's header is ONE tinted block. The tint was painted on three
   separate children — the title link, the meta foot and the body — so the card's
   own lighter ground showed through as white bars between them and around them,
   and which bars you got depended on which rows that story happened to have
   (#img1358-1360). Paint it once on the card; the panel below keeps its own
   lighter ground and now bleeds to the card's edges rather than floating inside
   its padding. */
/* The open state is stated twice in this file — as `.news-card--open` and as
   `:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"])`, which is a class and an attribute
   deeper. Both forms, both matched, or the per-child tints keep winning. */
/* `body` counts too: the card's white ground in tab mode is stated as
   `body.tt-on.home-search .home-v2 #section-newsfeed .news-card` — same class
   count as this, plus one type selector, which breaks the tie. */
body #section-newsfeed .news-card--open[class][class],
body #section-newsfeed .news-card[class][class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) {
  background: #eef2fa !important;
  padding-bottom: 0 !important;
}
#section-newsfeed .news-card--open[class] > .news-card-link,
#section-newsfeed .news-card--open[class] > .news-card-foot,
#section-newsfeed .news-card--open[class] > .news-card-body,
#section-newsfeed .news-card[class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) > .news-card-link,
#section-newsfeed .news-card[class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) > .news-card-foot,
#section-newsfeed .news-card[class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) > .news-card-body {
  background: transparent !important;
}
/* The actions row sat ON the rule that closes the header. */
#section-newsfeed .news-card--open[class] > .news-card-body,
#section-newsfeed .news-card[class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) > .news-card-body {
  padding-bottom: 12px !important;
}
#section-newsfeed .news-card[class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) > .news-panel,
#section-newsfeed .news-card--open[class] > .news-panel {
  background: #f8fafc !important;
  border-top: 1px solid #cfdaee !important;
}
/* The panel's ground reaches the card's edges by cancelling the card's own side
   padding. revamp1187 hard-coded that as -16, which is right for a bordered
   CARD (padding 14px 16px 12px) and wrong for a ROW, whose side padding is 0 —
   there the -16 pushed the panel OUTSIDE the card and left the insight text
   riding the screen edge (#img1376). Bleed only where there is padding to
   cancel; a row's panel is already flush and its content lines up with the
   story title above it. */
@media (min-width: 900px) {
  #section-newsfeed .news-card[class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) > .news-panel,
  #section-newsfeed .news-card--open[class] > .news-panel {
    margin-left: -16px !important;
    margin-right: -16px !important;
    padding-left: 16px !important;
    padding-right: 16px !important;
  }
}
@media (max-width: 899.98px) {
  /* A row carries no side padding of its own — its TEXT is inset 15px by the
     link and meta inside it (title measured at 32.5 against a content box
     starting at 17.5). The panel matches that inset so the insight lines up
     with the headline above it, and takes no negative margin, since there is
     nothing to cancel. */
  #section-newsfeed .news-card[class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) > .news-panel,
  #section-newsfeed .news-card--open[class] > .news-panel {
    margin-left: 0 !important;
    margin-right: 0 !important;
    padding-left: 15px !important;
    padding-right: 15px !important;
  }
}

/* (3) The briefing's foot rule was doubling: the last story block already ends
   on its own hairline, and the foot bar drew a second one 16px under it
   (#img1361). The bar keeps the spacing, the story block keeps the line. */
.dih-brief-footbar { border-top: 0 !important; }

/* (4) The separator between date and time reads centred. Geometrically it
   already was — but the run to its LEFT ends in text while the run to its RIGHT
   starts with an icon whose glyph is inset inside its box, so equal gaps put the
   dot visibly nearer the date (#img1362/1363). Give the text side ~3px more and
   the two read even. */
.di-mast-when { column-gap: 13px !important; }
.di-mast-date + .di-mast-time::before,
.di-mast-topic ~ .di-mast-time::before,
.di-mast-date ~ .di-mast-time::before { margin-right: 9px !important; }
.tdi-date .tdi-stamp-sep { margin-left: 3px !important; }

/* ═══ revamp1187d — one "Change Page" trigger, not one per page ══════════════
   The markup is shared, but its chevron rendered at its 18px attribute size on
   the pagenav bands and at 14 on Home, where `.tsp-btn-browse .tsp-chev` sizes
   it — so the same control looked different from page to page on a phone. One
   size, stated at the control. */
.tsp-btn-browse .tsp-chev[class] {
  width: 14px !important;
  height: 14px !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1188 — Home's tab strip is never a passenger in the grey band ════

   The band-plus-strip stack is written inside `@media (max-width: 1199.98px)`,
   from when tab mode could only happen below that width. revamp1187 moved
   Home's threshold to 1024 CONTENT px, so with the sidebar docked Home can now
   be in tab mode at a 1200-1400px viewport — outside that media query, where
   #sub-header is still a 56px flex ROW and the strip rides in it beside the
   page name and Change Page (#img1364). State the stack for tab mode itself,
   not for a width. No padding here: the strip's gutter is revamp1178/1179's. */
body.home-search.tt-on #sub-header.is-home-ident,
body.nav-docked.home-search.tt-on #sub-header.is-home-ident {
  display: block !important;
  height: auto !important;
  min-height: 0 !important;
}
body.home-search.tt-on #sub-header.is-home-ident .topic-banner {
  min-height: 56px !important;
  display: flex !important;
  align-items: center !important;
}
body.home-search.tt-on #sub-header .home-viewtabs[class] {
  display: flex !important;
  width: 100% !important;
  margin: 0 !important;
  align-items: center !important;
  background: #f8f9fc !important;
  border-top: 1px solid #e5e9f2 !important;
  border-bottom: 1px solid #e5e9f2 !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1189 — Featured Briefings are cards, and they fill their rows ════

   (1) The by-topic lists render each briefing as a bordered card; Featured
   rendered the same markup as bare columns of text, so the page's two halves
   looked like two different components (#img1365-1367). Same card here — the
   by-topic values exactly, so they stay one component. */
body #content .dih .dih-group--featured .dih-item {
  border: 1px solid #e6ebf3 !important;
  border-radius: 12px !important;
  background: #fbfcfe !important;
  padding: 13.6px 16px 15.2px !important;
  margin: 0 !important;
  box-shadow: none !important;
}
body #content .dih .dih-group--featured .dih-items {
  gap: 16px 25.6px !important;
}

/* (2) The set fills whole rows instead of leaving a ragged one: 8 across four
   columns, 6 across three, 4 across two. Fourteen are always rendered (tab mode
   runs the lot in one column) and the tail is hidden per layout — a display:none
   card never intersects, so the ones you can't see cost no fetch.
   nth-of-TYPE, not nth-child: an open briefing panel is also a child of this
   grid, and counting it would shift the cut by one. */
body:not(.tt-on) #content .dih .dih-group--featured .dih-items > button.dih-item {
  display: flex !important;
}
body:not(.tt-on) #content .dih .dih-group--featured .dih-items > button.dih-item:nth-of-type(n+9) {
  display: none !important;
}
/* Three columns between 1100 and 1400: the four-column grid at those widths
   left ~260px columns, which is where the summaries start breaking badly. */
@media (min-width: 1100px) and (max-width: 1399.98px) {
  .dih-group.dih-group--featured .dih-items {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)) !important;
  }
  body:not(.tt-on) #content .dih .dih-group--featured .dih-items > button.dih-item:nth-of-type(n+7) {
    display: none !important;
  }
}
@media (max-width: 1099.98px) {
  body:not(.tt-on) #content .dih .dih-group--featured .dih-items > button.dih-item:nth-of-type(n+5) {
    display: none !important;
  }
}

/* Related Coverage closes the briefing: the sources that matched no single
   story, shown as what they are rather than pinned under a claim (revamp1190). */
.di-related { margin-top: 22px !important; }
.di-related .dib-srcs { margin-top: 10px !important; }

/* ═══ revamp1193 — the subnav's topic glyph sits on the title's optical centre ══

   `.topic-subnav-inner .subnav-ident-ico { transform: translateY(2.5px) }` was
   added when the icon "read ~3px too high" against the name — but the row is
   `align-items: center` and the type has since changed, so the nudge now
   overshoots in the other direction. Measured on /topic/physics at 600px:
   glyph centre 86.0, the name's line-box centre 83.5, and its cap-band centre
   (cap top to baseline, which is what the eye reads as the middle of a word)
   ~83.8. The nudge is what put it 2px low.

   Flex centring alone lands the glyph at 83.5 — within half a pixel of the cap
   band — because the ascender space above the caps and the descender space
   below happen to balance at this size. So take the nudge out rather than
   replace one magic number with another. */
.topic-subnav-inner .subnav-ident-ico,
#sub-header .topic-subnav-inner .subnav-ident-ico {
  transform: none !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1195 — the Search page: one seam to the nav, and results that lead ══

   Three layouts, three different bugs, one cause: #content's top reserve was
   written for a page whose first element is white. The search page opens on a
   blue hero band, so any reserve that doesn't exactly match the visible chrome
   shows as a white stripe above the band — or, when the reserve is too small,
   swallows the title behind the bar (#img1370-1373):

     docked           reserve 0    → title UNDER the grey band
     wide, collapsed  reserve 78   → 22px of white between nav and band
     tab mode         flush        → title behind the band

   Reserve exactly what is on screen, and put the breathing room INSIDE the hero
   where it belongs — so the band always meets the chrome, and the title always
   has the same air under it whatever is above. */
body.custom-mode.nav-docked[class][class] #content {
  padding-top: var(--subnav-height, 56px) !important;
}
body.custom-mode:not(.nav-docked).tt-on[class][class] #content {
  padding-top: calc(var(--nav-h) + var(--subnav-height, 56px)) !important;
}
body.custom-mode:not(.nav-docked):not(.tt-on)[class][class] #content {
  padding-top: var(--nav-h) !important;
}
/* The air the title needs, stated once, in the hero. */
body.custom-mode .search-panel-hero,
body.custom-mode .search-page .search-panel-hero {
  padding-top: 30px !important;
}

/* The query, echoed under the bar in place of the old results header. */
.search-echo {
  margin: 10px 0 0 !important;
  font-family: var(--font-family);
  font-size: 0.95rem;
  font-style: italic;
  color: #6b7a96;
  text-align: center;
}
.search-echo[hidden] { display: none !important; }

/* The tab strip closes on a rule, and the first result sits ON it rather than
   drawing a second one (or none — Prompts had a doubled line, News had no top
   border at all, Web Resources had one; #img1374/1375).

   revamp1196: the rule belongs on `.search-topzone-tabs` — the strip you can
   actually see. `.aii-flatnav` is the builder's own nav, which this surface
   renders display:none, so the first pass drew its 2px line on nothing. */
.search-stickzone,
.search-topzone-tabs[class] {
  border-bottom: 2px solid #e0e5ee !important;
  margin-bottom: 0 !important;
}
.search-topzone-tabs[class] {
  padding-bottom: 10px !important;
  border-bottom: 0 !important;
}
/* Whatever the active tab renders first sits on that rule and adds nothing of
   its own — three surfaces that each had a different answer. */
.search-panel-results .aii-builder-secs > *:first-child,
.search-panel-results .aii-fi-acclist > .aii-fi-acc:first-child,
.search-panel-results .aii-fi-acclist > .aii-fi-acc:first-child > .aii-fi-accsum,
.search-panel-results .news-list > .news-card:first-child,
.search-panel-results .pdir > *:first-child,
.search-panel-results .aii-ext-block:first-child,
.search-panel-results .search-news-section .news-list > .news-card:first-child {
  border-top: 0 !important;
  margin-top: 0 !important;
}
.search-panel-results .aii-builder-secs,
.search-panel-results .aii-fi-acclist { padding-top: 0 !important; margin-top: 0 !important; }
/* News keeps a 6px lead-in of its own — Prompts and Web Resources land at 0. */
.search-panel-results .search-news-section .news-list,
.search-panel-results .news-list { padding-top: 0 !important; }

/* ═══ revamp1196 — the query and the tabs stay with the results ═════════════
   Scrolling past the hero left the grey band saying "Search" and nothing else:
   no query, no indication of which of the three kinds you were reading. The
   echo and the tab strip now stick together directly under whatever chrome is
   on screen — the band when it's there, the nav when it isn't. */
/* Sticky sticks only within its PARENT's box, so this lives as a sibling of the
   results rather than inside .search-panel-topfold — inside that short fold it
   scrolled away with the hero (measured: -397px at scrollY 700) instead of
   pinning. Its parent is now .search-panel, whose box spans the results. */
.search-stickzone {
  position: sticky;
  z-index: 40;
  background: #fff;
  padding-top: 6px;
}
body.custom-mode.nav-docked .search-stickzone {
  top: var(--subnav-height, 56px);
}
body.custom-mode:not(.nav-docked) .search-stickzone {
  top: calc(var(--nav-h) + var(--subnav-height, 56px));
}
/* Over the hero's blue it should read as part of the hero, not a white slab —
   it only needs its own ground once it is actually stuck over results. */
.search-panel[data-state="collapsed"] .search-stickzone { background: transparent; }

/* ═══ revamp1198 — the AI Briefing is an edition ════════════════════════════

   Both cards — the collapsed preview and the open briefing — read as one
   masthead now: topic pill → "<Topic> Briefing" → date | time | AI-generated,
   piped. Under the masthead the open briefing runs a "Today in Focus" panel
   (the three lead points, three columns with vertical rules in a wide column,
   stacked with hairlines in a narrow one), the summary paragraph on its own,
   then "Top Stories" — each story's text on the left with its SOURCES in a
   ruled right-hand column when the container is wide enough, inline beneath
   it otherwise. Which chips appear is unchanged (revamp1190/1191); only where.

   The title face: a serif, from the system. The site sets every heading in
   General Sans, so a General Sans title here would be one more heading; the
   reference's high-contrast serif is what makes the briefing read as an
   edition. Iowan Old Style (macOS/iOS) → Palatino Linotype / Book Antiqua
   (Windows) → Georgia → the platform serif: no webfont, no request, and one
   token to swap if a self-hosted display serif is ever wanted. */
/* revamp1207: no serif. The Playfair masthead (revamp1202) was rejected — it
   read as a different product from the geometric sans everything else is set
   in. The title is General Sans again, and what makes it a masthead rather
   than one more heading is stated in the revamp1207 block at the end of this
   file: heavier, larger, tightly tracked, and two-tone (navy into the accent
   blue) via a gradient clipped to the glyphs. One token still swaps every
   surface. */
:root { --font-brief-title: var(--font-display, 'General Sans', Inter, sans-serif); }

/* ── Masthead ── */
/* [class]×3: `.tdi-card--hero2.is-open .di-page .di-mast` (0,4,0) and
   `body.tt-on .tview-brief .di-mast` (0,3,1) both state margins with
   !important; this has to outrank both. */
.di-page .di-mast.di-mast--v2[class][class][class] {
  display: flex !important; flex-direction: column !important; align-items: flex-start !important;
  flex-wrap: nowrap !important; gap: 10px !important; margin: 0 0 6px !important;
  font-size: 1rem !important;
}
.di-mast--v2 .di-mast-topic[class] {
  flex-basis: auto !important; margin: 0 !important;
  padding: 5px 11px !important; border-radius: 7px !important;
  font-size: 0.7rem !important; letter-spacing: 0.09em !important;
}
.di-mast--v2 .di-title {
  margin: 0 !important; padding: 0 !important;
  font-family: var(--font-brief-title) !important;
  font-weight: 700 !important;
  font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 1.1rem + 2.3cqi, 2.5rem) !important;
  line-height: 1.08 !important; letter-spacing: -0.012em !important;
  color: #101a33 !important;
  text-wrap: balance;
}
.di-metaline {
  display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: 8px 12px;
  width: 100%; min-width: 0;
}
.di-metagrp { display: inline-flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: 8px 12px; }
.di-meta-date, .di-meta-time {
  display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 6px; white-space: nowrap;
  font-family: var(--font-family); font-size: 0.84rem; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.2; color: #5c6a85;
}
.di-meta-date svg, .di-meta-time svg { width: 15px; height: 15px; flex: none; opacity: .8; }
.di-metasep { display: inline-block; width: 1px; height: 14px; background: #c9d2e1; flex: none; }
/* The AI-generated pill keeps revamp1186's shape; it just loses its stack
   margins (0.4rem from the base rule, 6/12 in tab mode) to sit on the line. */
/* flex-start, not center: in the narrow column the line stacks, and center
   would then centre the pill HORIZONTALLY. On one row the pill is the tallest
   item, so start and centre coincide. */
.di-metaline .di-prov2[class][class][class][class] { margin: 0 !important; align-self: flex-start !important; }
/* Narrow column: date | time on one line, the AI-generated pill under it. */
@container (max-width: 479px) {
  .di-metaline { flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; gap: 9px; }
  .di-metasep--prov { display: none; }
}
/* In tab mode the topic pill is gone (the subnav names the topic) and there is
   no ✕ to clear, so the masthead takes back the 38px reserved for it. */
body.tt-on .tview-brief [data-di-host] .di-mast--v2[class] { padding-right: 0 !important; }
/* Tab mode keeps the pill (the mobile reference leads with it) but a tab-mode
   rule gives it an 8px margin of its own on top of the masthead's 10px gap. */
body.tt-on .tview-brief .di-mast--v2 .di-mast-topic[class][class] { margin: 0 !important; }

/* ── Section labels: small blue caps; the rule variant runs a line to the right ── */
.di-lbl {
  margin: 0; padding: 0; border: 0;
  font-family: var(--font-family) !important; font-size: 0.69rem !important; font-weight: 750 !important;
  letter-spacing: 0.11em !important; text-transform: uppercase; line-height: 1.2 !important;
  color: #2d54b4 !important;
}
.di-lbl--rule { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 14px; }
.di-lbl--rule::after { content: ""; flex: 1 1 auto; height: 1px; background: #dfe5ef; }

/* ── Today in Focus ── */
.di-focus {
  margin: 0 0 8px !important; padding: 16px 20px 17px;
  border-radius: 14px; background: #eef2f9; border: 1px solid #e2e8f3;
}
.di-focus-lbl { margin-bottom: 12px !important; }
.di-focus-list { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
.di-focus-list li {
  min-width: 0; padding: 10px 0; border-top: 1px solid #d3dcec;
  font-family: var(--font-family); font-size: 0.95rem; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.45; color: #172444;
}
.di-focus-list li:first-child { border-top: 0; padding-top: 0; }
.di-focus-list li:last-child { padding-bottom: 0; }
@container (min-width: 640px) {
  .di-focus { padding: 16px 22px 18px; }
  .di-focus-list { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); column-gap: 22px; }
  .di-focus-list li { padding: 0 0 0 22px; border-top: 0; border-left: 1px solid #cfd8e8; }
  .di-focus-list li:first-child { padding-left: 0; border-left: 0; }
}

/* ── The summary, on its own under the panel ── */
.di-summary { font-family: var(--font-family); font-size: 0.98rem; line-height: 1.66; color: #2b3853; }
.di-summary p { margin: 0 0 0.8em; }
.di-summary p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

/* ── Top Stories ── */
.di-briefs.di-briefs--v2[class] { margin-top: 16px !important; }
.di-briefs--v2 .di-lbl--rule { margin-bottom: 4px; }
.di-briefs.di-briefs--v2 .dib[class] {
  display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 10px;
  margin: 0 !important; padding: 15px 0 16px !important; border-top: 1px solid #e8edf5 !important;
}
/* The label is the section's first child, so `:first-child` never reaches
   the first story; it is the label's next sibling. */
.di-briefs.di-briefs--v2 .di-lbl + .dib[class] { margin-top: 0 !important; padding-top: 12px !important; border-top: 0 !important; }
.di-briefs.di-briefs--v2 .dib:last-child[class] { border-bottom: 1px solid #e8edf5 !important; }
.dib-main { min-width: 0; }
.dib--v2 .dib-head[class] {
  margin: 0 0 6px !important;
  font-family: var(--font-display) !important; font-size: 1.02rem !important; font-weight: 700 !important;
  letter-spacing: -0.012em !important; line-height: 1.3 !important; color: #14204a !important;
}
.dib--v2 .dib-body { font-size: 0.93rem; line-height: 1.6; color: #34405a; }
.dib-side { min-width: 0; }
.dib-side .dib-srcs { margin-top: 0; }
@container (min-width: 700px) {
  .di-briefs.di-briefs--v2 .dib[class] { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 200px; column-gap: 24px; }
  .dib-side { border-left: 1px solid #e3e9f3; padding-left: 22px; align-self: stretch; }
  .dib-side .dib-srcs { flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; gap: 8px; }
  .dib-side .dib-srcs-label { margin: 0 0 1px; }
  .dib-side .dib-src { max-width: 100%; }
}
.di-related .di-lbl--rule { margin-bottom: 2px; }

/* ── The preview card carries the same masthead, scaled down ── */
.tdi-mast2 { display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; gap: 9px; margin: 0 0 12px; min-width: 0; width: 100%; }
/* (0,5,0): the evening edition recolours the pill's text at
   `.topic-home .tdi-card--hero2.is-evening .tdi-topiclabel` (0,4,0 !important). */
.tdi-card .tdi-mast2 .tdi-topiclabel--inline[class][class] {
  margin: 0 !important; padding: 5px 11px !important; border-radius: 7px !important;
  font-size: 0.7rem !important; letter-spacing: 0.09em !important; line-height: 1.2 !important; color: #1e3a8a !important;
}
.tdi-card .tdi-brieftitle[class] {
  display: block !important; margin: 0 !important; padding: 0 !important;
  font-family: var(--font-brief-title) !important; font-weight: 700 !important;
  font-size: 1.5rem !important; line-height: 1.12 !important; letter-spacing: -0.01em !important;
  color: #101a33 !important; text-wrap: balance;
}
.tdi-metaline { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: 8px 12px; width: 100%; min-width: 0; }
.tdi-metaline .tdi-date[class][class] {
  display: inline-flex !important; align-items: center !important; gap: 10px !important; margin: 0 !important; padding: 0 !important;
  font-size: 0.82rem !important; font-weight: 600 !important; color: #5c6a85 !important;
  letter-spacing: 0 !important; text-transform: none !important; line-height: 1.2 !important; white-space: nowrap;
}
.tdi-metaline .tdi-stamp-val[class][class] {
  display: inline-flex !important; align-items: center !important; gap: 6px !important;
  font-size: 0.82rem !important; font-weight: 600 !important; color: #5c6a85 !important;
  letter-spacing: 0 !important; text-transform: none !important; line-height: 1.2 !important; margin: 0 !important; padding: 0 !important;
}
.tdi-metaline .tdi-stamp-val svg { width: 14px !important; height: 14px !important; opacity: .8; }
.tdi-metaline .tdi-stamp-sep[class][class],
.tdi-metaline .tdi-metasep {
  display: inline-block !important; width: 1px !important; height: 14px !important; flex: none;
  margin: 0 !important; padding: 0 !important; background: #c9d2e1 !important; color: transparent !important; font-size: 0 !important;
  border-radius: 0 !important; opacity: 1 !important;
}
/* 5 classes: revamp1086 states the label's padding/gap at `.tdi-card .di-prov2`
   and revamp1186's pill at `.tdi-cardprov[class][class][class]`; the margin
   below it is stated with !important elsewhere. */
.tdi-metaline .tdi-cardprov[class][class][class][class] { margin: 0 !important; align-self: flex-start !important; }
/* The card's column is narrow (the topic rail is ~340px): date | time on one
   line, the AI-generated pill under it. */
.tdi-card .tdi-metaline { flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; gap: 9px; }
.tdi-card .tdi-metaline .tdi-metasep--prov { display: none !important; }
/* The masthead's title used to be the hidden "Your 5-minute AI briefing" h3 —
   that rule targets .tdi-today-title, which the new title does not carry. */
.tdi-card--hero2 .tdi-summary[class] { margin: 0 0 13px !important; }

/* ═══ revamp1203 — an open story takes the whole row, and the first one stops
   sitting lower than the rest ═══════════════════════════════════════════════

   (1) The insight opened inside its own grid column, so on the two-column feed
   it rendered in half the width available while the column beside it sat empty
   (#img1377). A briefing wants the full measure: the open card spans both
   columns and the cards around it reflow, which is also what makes the panel's
   line length readable. */
#section-newsfeed .news-list .news-card--open,
#section-newsfeed .news-list .news-card:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) {
  grid-column: 1 / -1 !important;
}

/* (2) The first story's title sat 27px into its card against 15px for every
   other one. `.news-card:first-child .news-card-link { padding-top: 0.95rem }`
   is a leftover from the flat-list layout, where the first row had to clear the
   head's rule and the card had no padding of its own to do it. As a bordered
   card it does, so the extra is pure offset (#img1378). Rows below 900 keep it —
   there the sub-header's rule really is the first story's top edge. */
@media (min-width: 900px) {
  /* `body` and a second [class]: the 12px is stated as
     `body.home-search #section-newsfeed … .news-card-link` — same class count,
     plus a type selector, and the type column breaks the tie. */
  body #section-newsfeed .news-list .news-card:first-child .news-card-link[class][class] {
    padding-top: 0 !important;
  }
}

/* revamp1203c — where an opened story comes to rest. scrollIntoView honours
   scroll-margin-top, and unlike a measurement taken at click time this is
   resolved against the SCROLLED state — the sticky band is revealed by
   scrolling, so measuring it beforehand under-reads it by its own height and
   parks the card behind it. */
#section-newsfeed .news-card { scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--nav-h) + var(--subnav-height, 56px) + 14px); }
body.nav-docked #section-newsfeed .news-card { scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--subnav-height, 56px) + 14px); }
/* Topic pages in tab mode carry a separate fixed strip that --subnav-height
   does not measure (see --tt-tabstrip-h). */
body.tt-on:not(.home-search) #section-newsfeed .news-card {
  scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--nav-h) + var(--subnav-height, 56px) + var(--tt-tabstrip-h, 56px) + 14px);
}
body.tt-on.nav-docked:not(.home-search) #section-newsfeed .news-card {
  scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--subnav-height, 56px) + var(--tt-tabstrip-h, 56px) + 14px);
}

/* ═══ revamp1204 — ONE definition for an opened story ═══════════════════════

   The open card's chrome had accumulated across a dozen rules that disagreed
   with each other, so what you saw depended on which page you were on, which
   position the card held, and how wide the window was (#img1379-1398):

     · the card lost its vertical padding, so the headline rode the top edge
     · the title and source line kept a 15px inset the card's own padding had
       already provided, so they sat right of the insight text beneath them
     · `.news-card-body` drew a bottom border AND the panel drew a top one —
       the phantom second line under Close Insight / View Story, on every page
     · the border was 1px #e8edf5 here and 1.5px #b9c6e4 there, and on some
       positions it vanished at the top entirely
     · rows below 900px kept their hairline separators around a card that now
       has a real border, so a line poked out beside it

   This states the whole thing once, for every surface (feed, topic page,
   search results), every position and both layouts. Read it as the definition;
   anything above that disagrees is history. */

/* ── The card ── */
body #section-newsfeed .news-card--open[class],
body #section-newsfeed .news-card[class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]),
body .search-news-section .news-card--open[class],
body .search-news-section .news-card[class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) {
  border: 1.5px solid #b9c6e4 !important;
  border-radius: 12px !important;
  overflow: hidden !important;
  background: #eef2fa !important;
  box-shadow: 0 6px 20px -14px rgba(30, 58, 138, 0.28) !important;
}
/* A bordered card supplies its own edges — the list's row hairlines must not
   draw around it, on either side. */
body #section-newsfeed .news-card--open[class]::after,
body #section-newsfeed .news-card[class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"])::after,
body #section-newsfeed .news-card[class]:has(+ .news-card--open)::after {
  display: none !important;
}

/* ── The header: title, source line, actions ── */
@media (min-width: 900px) {
  body #section-newsfeed .news-card--open[class],
  body #section-newsfeed .news-card[class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) {
    padding: 14px 16px 0 !important;
  }
  /* The card already insets its contents; the link and foot must not inset them
     again, or the headline sits right of the insight text below it. */
  body #section-newsfeed .news-card--open[class] > .news-card-link,
  body #section-newsfeed .news-card--open[class] > .news-card-foot,
  body #section-newsfeed .news-card--open[class] > .news-card-body,
  body #section-newsfeed .news-card[class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) > .news-card-link,
  body #section-newsfeed .news-card[class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) > .news-card-foot,
  body #section-newsfeed .news-card[class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) > .news-card-body {
    padding-left: 0 !important;
    padding-right: 0 !important;
  }
}
@media (max-width: 899.98px) {
  /* Rows carry no side padding of their own — the 15px inset lives on the link
     and foot, and the panel matches it (revamp1197b). */
  body #section-newsfeed .news-card--open[class],
  body #section-newsfeed .news-card[class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) {
    padding: 14px 0 0 !important;
  }
}

/* ── The seam between header and insight: ONE rule, the panel's. ── */
body #section-newsfeed .news-card--open[class] > .news-card-body,
body #section-newsfeed .news-card[class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) > .news-card-body,
body .search-news-section .news-card--open[class] > .news-card-body {
  border-bottom: 0 !important;
  padding-bottom: 12px !important;
}

/* ── Section headings inside the insight ──
   They were 12.8px against 15px body copy — SMALLER than the text they head,
   which is why the structure reads flat. Up to 0.95rem and the site's navy. */
.ni-sec-name[class] {
  font-size: 0.95rem !important;
  font-weight: 700 !important;
  color: #1e3a8a !important;
  letter-spacing: -0.005em;
}

/* ── Hover ──
   A story card had a background transition and nothing else, so on a bordered
   card there was no hover at all. The border and a shallow lift carry it; the
   two actions get their own. */
@media (hover: hover) {
  body #section-newsfeed .news-card:not(.news-card--open):hover,
  body .search-news-section .news-card:not(.news-card--open):hover {
    border-color: #c3d0e8 !important;
    box-shadow: 0 4px 14px -10px rgba(30, 58, 138, 0.35) !important;
  }
  body #section-newsfeed .news-card .news-act:hover,
  body .search-news-section .news-card .news-act:hover {
    background: #eef2fa !important;
    border-color: #b9c6e4 !important;
    color: #14213d !important;
  }
}
#section-newsfeed .news-card,
.search-news-section .news-card {
  transition: background .13s ease, border-color .13s ease, box-shadow .16s ease !important;
}
#section-newsfeed .news-card .news-act,
.search-news-section .news-card .news-act {
  transition: background .12s ease, border-color .12s ease, color .12s ease !important;
}

/* ── Full width wherever the feed is a grid ── */
.search-news-section .news-list .news-card--open,
.search-news-section .news-list .news-card:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) {
  grid-column: 1 / -1 !important;
}

/* revamp1204b — …and the FIRST story, which had its own rules.
   Several `:first-child` rules (revamp1179/1181h) pin that card's top border
   and padding for the CLOSED state, at five classes plus a type selector. Open,
   they beat the definition above and left story one alone looking like the old
   card: a 1px grey top border and the headline riding the edge. Same values,
   stated for the position that has its own history. */
body #section-newsfeed .news-list > .news-card--open:first-child[class][class][class][class],
body #section-newsfeed .news-list > .news-card:first-child[class][class][class][class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) {
  border: 1.5px solid #b9c6e4 !important;
  border-radius: 12px !important;
}
@media (min-width: 900px) {
  body #section-newsfeed .news-list > .news-card--open:first-child[class][class][class][class],
  body #section-newsfeed .news-list > .news-card:first-child[class][class][class][class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) {
    padding: 14px 16px 0 !important;
  }
}
@media (max-width: 899.98px) {
  body #section-newsfeed .news-list > .news-card--open:first-child[class][class][class][class],
  body #section-newsfeed .news-list > .news-card:first-child[class][class][class][class]:has(.news-act-ai[aria-expanded="true"]) {
    padding: 14px 0 0 !important;
  }
}

/* ═══ revamp1205 — ONE accordion card for the three directories ═════════════

   All Topics (Topics page), Briefings by Topic (AI Briefings hub) and Prompts by
   Topic (Prompts page) had drifted to hairline rows — revamp1108b/1116/1126
   flattened three separately-built accordions, and each only grew its box when
   opened. They are cards again, and the same card: a 1px border, 12px corners,
   a 10px row gap, a 58px head with 14px side padding, and the open one darkens
   its border, tints its head and spans the row (the base rules already give it
   `grid-column: 1 / -1`). The `#content` prefix out-specifies every earlier
   `.aiidd-tree .aiidd-parent` / `.dih .dih-group:not(...)` / `.ph-body .pdir
   .pdir-card` rule (all class-only), including the open-state ones and the
   Prompts `is-aboveopen` separator hide — a bordered card keeps every edge. */
#content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent,
#content .dih .dih-groups > .dih-group:not(.dih-group--featured),
#content .ph-body .pdir > .pdir-card {
  border: 1px solid #dfe5ef !important;
  border-radius: 12px !important;
  background: #fff !important;
  box-shadow: none !important;
  margin: 0 !important;
  padding: 0 !important;
  overflow: hidden !important;
  align-self: start;
  transition: border-color .14s ease, box-shadow .14s ease;
}
/* The grids: a real gap between cards (three earlier rules zeroed the row gap
   so hairline rows would sit on their separators), and a little air under the
   section head's rule so the first card doesn't butt into it. */
#content .ndp .aiidd-tree,
#content .dih .dih-groups,
#content .ph-lib .ph-body .pdir {
  row-gap: 10px !important;
  column-gap: 12px !important;
  margin-top: 12px !important;
  padding-top: 0 !important;
}
/* Heads: one height, one inset. */
#content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent > .aiidd-parent-head,
#content .dih .dih-groups > .dih-group:not(.dih-group--featured) > .dih-grouphead,
#content .ph-body .pdir > .pdir-card > .pdir-cardhead {
  display: flex !important;
  align-items: center !important;
  min-height: 58px !important;
  padding: 0 14px !important;
  border-radius: 0 !important;
  border-bottom: 0 !important;
  margin: 0 !important;
}
/* The flat parent (no subtopics) is a link, not a button — same row height. */
#content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent.aiidd-parent-flat {
  display: flex !important;
  align-items: center !important;
  min-height: 58px !important;
  padding: 0 14px !important;
}
@media (hover: hover) {
  #content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[data-open="false"]:hover,
  #content .dih .dih-groups > .dih-group:not(.dih-group--featured):not(.is-open):hover,
  #content .ph-body .pdir > .pdir-card:not(.is-open):hover {
    border-color: #b9c6e4 !important;
  }
  #content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent.aiidd-parent-flat:hover { border-color: #b9c6e4 !important; }
}
/* Open: darker edge, tinted head, a soft lift; the head's rule closes it off
   from the body. No extra margin — the grid gap is the spacing now. */
#content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[data-open="true"],
#content .dih .dih-groups > .dih-group:not(.dih-group--featured).is-open,
#content .ph-body .pdir > .pdir-card.is-open {
  border-color: #b9c6e4 !important;
  box-shadow: 0 8px 24px -14px rgba(30, 58, 138, 0.30) !important;
  grid-column: 1 / -1;
}
#content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[data-open="true"] > .aiidd-parent-head,
#content .dih .dih-groups > .dih-group:not(.dih-group--featured).is-open > .dih-grouphead,
#content .ph-body .pdir > .pdir-card.is-open > .pdir-cardhead {
  background: #eef2fa !important;
  border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, #2f4d9e 12%, transparent) !important;
}
/* The Topics subtopic list sat 6px in from a borderless row; inside a card it
   lines up under the head's 14px inset (the list carries its own 16px). */
#content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent > .aiidd-parent-body { padding: 6px 4px 8px !important; }

/* Topics page in tab mode: the section heads come back exactly as the wide
   layout draws them (the small-caps condensing and the tab-strip hides are
   gone above). The switch keeps its place at the head's right edge. */
body.tt-on #content .ndp .aiidd-pagehead .aiidd-expandall[class] { display: inline-flex !important; }

/* revamp1205b — tab mode, Topics: the first section head sat on the band.
   The tab strip used to be the thing under the band; with the heads back the
   Featured Topics title rode the band's bottom edge (2px). Same breathing room
   the wide layout gives it, and the head glyphs come back too (their tab-mode
   hide is removed in place above) — it IS the wide layout, one column. */
body.tt-on #content .ndp .tfeat-section > .aiidd-pagehead.tfeat-head[class] { padding-top: 18px !important; }

/* ═══ revamp1207 — the masthead in the site's own face; a colour per topic;
   section-header chips in the label pill's blues; topic heads match home ═════

   ── 1. "<Topic> Briefing" ──
   The serif is gone (see --font-brief-title). What makes this line the
   masthead of a daily edition, rather than one more General Sans heading, is
   weight and setting rather than a typeface: 700 (the heaviest weight the site
   ships), a size up, tracking pulled in to -0.032em so the words lock into one
   block, and a two-tone run from the site navy into the accent blue — a
   gradient clipped to the glyphs, so it needs no markup (the expanded title is
   rendered by a component this pass does not edit) and both surfaces share
   one rule. `width: fit-content` makes the gradient span the words, not the
   column, so the blue always lands on the last word ("Briefing"). Specificity:
   the expanded title is set at `.di-mast--v2 .di-title` (0,2,0) and the card's
   at `.tdi-card .tdi-brieftitle[class]` (0,3,0), both !important. */
.di-mast--v2 .di-title[class],
.tdi-card .tdi-brieftitle[class][class] {
  font-family: var(--font-brief-title) !important;
  font-weight: 700 !important;
  letter-spacing: -0.032em !important;
  line-height: 1.04 !important;
  width: fit-content; max-width: 100%;
  background: linear-gradient(98deg, #14213d 0%, #14213d 34%, #1e3a8a 100%);
  -webkit-background-clip: text; background-clip: text;
  color: transparent !important; -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
  text-wrap: balance;
}
.di-mast--v2 .di-title[class] { font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 1.2rem + 2.6cqi, 2.8rem) !important; }
.tdi-card .tdi-brieftitle[class][class] { font-size: 1.62rem !important; }

/* ── 2. A colour per topic ──
   Every parent topic carries `color` in data/topics.json; the markup sets it
   as `style="--tc: …"` on the hero chip, each subtopic pill and each directory
   card (topicColorStyle in js/utils/data.js — a subtopic gets its parent's hue
   at 86%, barely lighter). Nothing below names a topic: one rule set reads
   var(--tc) and mixes its tints. `[style*="--tc"]` both scopes the rules to
   elements that have a scheme and adds the class-level specificity needed to
   clear the older !important chip rules (revamp1129/1133/1139/1205). */

/* Topic hero: the topic's colour with a white glyph (was solid #1e3a8a at
   `body:not(.tt-on) .tbh-titleic` (0,2,1) !important). */
.topic-bodyhead .tbh-titleic[class][style*="--tc"] { background: var(--tc) !important; color: #fff !important; }

/* Related-topic pills, wide layout: the topic's icon beside its name, in the
   topic's colour. The pill is already inline-flex with a 6px gap. Hidden in tab
   mode (the strips there are untouched by design). */
.tbh-sub .tbh-sub-ic { display: none; }
body:not(.tt-on) .topic-bodyhead .tbh-sub .tbh-sub-ic {
  display: inline-grid; place-items: center; flex: none;
  width: 16px; height: 16px; margin-left: -2px;
  color: var(--tc, #4a5f8f);
}
.tbh-sub .tbh-sub-ic svg { display: block; width: 15px; height: 15px; }
@media (hover: hover) {
  body:not(.tt-on) .topic-bodyhead .tbh-sub:hover .tbh-sub-ic { color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tc, #1d4ed8) 82%, #000); }
}

/* Directory cards — All Topics, Briefings by Topic, Prompts by Topic. Closed:
   the head chip is the topic's colour with a white glyph. Open: the head takes
   a faint tint of the colour, its border and lift follow, and the chip goes a
   shade darker. The rows beneath carry the scheme in their glyphs and (Topics)
   their names, as bare glyphs — no chip. Row hovers deepen the same hue. */
#content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[style*="--tc"] .aiidd-parent-ic[class],
#content .dih .dih-groups > .dih-group[style*="--tc"] .dih-group-ic[class],
#content .ph-body .pdir > .pdir-card[style*="--tc"] .pdir-card-ic[class] {
  background: var(--tc) !important;
  color: #fff !important;
}
#content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[style*="--tc"][data-open="true"],
#content .dih .dih-groups > .dih-group[style*="--tc"].is-open,
#content .ph-body .pdir > .pdir-card[style*="--tc"].is-open {
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tc) 42%, #dfe5ef) !important;
  box-shadow: 0 8px 24px -14px color-mix(in srgb, var(--tc) 45%, transparent) !important;
}
#content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[style*="--tc"][data-open="true"] > .aiidd-parent-head[class],
#content .dih .dih-groups > .dih-group[style*="--tc"].is-open > .dih-grouphead[class],
#content .ph-body .pdir > .pdir-card[style*="--tc"].is-open > .pdir-cardhead[class] {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tc) 7%, #fff) !important;
  border-bottom-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tc) 22%, transparent) !important;
}
#content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[style*="--tc"][data-open="true"] .aiidd-parent-ic[class],
#content .dih .dih-groups > .dih-group[style*="--tc"].is-open .dih-group-ic[class],
#content .ph-body .pdir > .pdir-card[style*="--tc"].is-open .pdir-card-ic[class] {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tc) 78%, #000) !important;
}
#content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[style*="--tc"][data-open="true"] .aiidd-parent-name,
#content .dih .dih-groups > .dih-group[style*="--tc"].is-open .dih-grouptitle,
#content .ph-body .pdir > .pdir-card[style*="--tc"].is-open .pdir-card-name[class] {
  color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tc) 72%, #14213d) !important;
}
/* The rows: glyphs in the colour, no chip. */
#content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[style*="--tc"] .aiidd-vlink-ic[class],
#content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[style*="--tc"] .aiidd-vall .aiidd-vlink-ic[class],
#content .dih .dih-groups > .dih-group[style*="--tc"] .dih-item-ic[class],
#content .ph-body .pdir > .pdir-card[style*="--tc"] .pdir-cell-ic[class] {
  color: var(--tc) !important;
  background: transparent !important;
}
#content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[style*="--tc"] .aiidd-vall[class],
#content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[style*="--tc"] .aiidd-vlink[class] {
  color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tc) 64%, #14213d) !important;
}
@media (hover: hover) {
  #content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[style*="--tc"] .aiidd-vall[class]:hover,
  #content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[style*="--tc"] .aiidd-vlink[class]:hover,
  #content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[style*="--tc"] .aiidd-vlink[class]:hover .aiidd-vlink-ic,
  #content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[style*="--tc"] .aiidd-vall[class]:hover .aiidd-vlink-ic,
  #content .dih .dih-groups > .dih-group[style*="--tc"] .dih-item:hover .dih-item-ic[class],
  #content .ph-body .pdir > .pdir-card[style*="--tc"] .pdir-cell:hover .pdir-cell-ic[class] {
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tc) 82%, #000) !important;
  }
  #content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[style*="--tc"][data-open="false"]:hover,
  #content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent.aiidd-parent-flat[style*="--tc"]:hover,
  #content .dih .dih-groups > .dih-group[style*="--tc"]:not(.is-open):hover,
  #content .ph-body .pdir > .pdir-card[style*="--tc"]:not(.is-open):hover {
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tc) 38%, #dfe5ef) !important;
  }
}

/* ── 3. Section-header chips: the briefing label pill's two blues ──
   Background = the pill's #bcd0f5, glyph = its text #1e3a8a. Chips only —
   the tab-mode strips (.pst-tab / .tvt) are not touched. Existing values sit
   at (0,1,0)–(0,3,1) with !important; a second [class] clears each. */
.home-v2 .home-side .hb-head-ic--brief[class],
.home-v2 .home-side .hb-head-ic--prompts[class],
.trail-head .trail-head-ic[class][class],
.ph-sec-ic[class][class],
.aiidd-head-ic[class][class] {
  background: #bcd0f5 !important;
  color: #1e3a8a !important;
}
.home-v2 .home-side .hb-head-ic--brief[class] svg,
.home-v2 .home-side .hb-head-ic--prompts[class] svg,
.trail-head .trail-head-ic[class][class] svg,
.ph-sec-ic[class][class] svg,
.aiidd-head-ic[class][class] svg { color: #1e3a8a !important; }
/* The sparkles are filled, the wand/grid are stroked; the older rules set
   each explicitly (#4f7fe0 / #2c4f9e !important), so match them per property. */
.home-v2 .home-side .hb-head-ic--brief[class] svg,
.trail-head .trail-head-ic--brief[class][class] svg { fill: #1e3a8a !important; }
.home-v2 .home-side .hb-head-ic--prompts[class] svg,
.trail-head .trail-head-ic--prompts[class][class] svg { stroke: #1e3a8a !important; }

/* ── 4. Topic-page "AI Briefing" / "AI Prompts" heads: the homepage's navy ──
   Home measures #1a2c66 (.home-v2 .home-side .hb-title); the topic rail read
   #16307a from `body:not(.tt-on) .topic-band .topic-home .trail-head` (0,5,1)
   !important under (min-width:1200px). Same path plus one class. */
.trail-head[class][class] { color: #1a2c66 !important; }
@media (min-width: 1200px) {
  body:not(.tt-on) .topic-band .topic-home .trail-head[class] { color: #1a2c66 !important; }
}

/* ═══ revamp1209 — the briefing masthead, third attempt ═════════════════════

   Playfair (1202) was a different product's typeface. The gradient-clipped
   General Sans that replaced it (1207) had the opposite problem: at 700 in
   navy-into-blue it is the SAME lockup as the "AI Briefing" section header
   sitting directly above it, so the card's own title read as a second header
   rather than a masthead — and the gradient did the differentiating work that
   ought to be structural (#img1399).

   What separates a masthead from a heading isn't colour, it's CONTRAST. The
   family already ships 300 and 700, so the two halves of the title carry
   different weights: the topic heavy, the word "Briefing" light beside it. No
   new font, no gradient, nothing borrowed from another component — and at a
   glance it cannot be mistaken for the section header above, which is one
   weight throughout. The old rule's `background-clip: text` is unset
   explicitly; left standing it would clip the new colours to nothing. */
.di-mast--v2 .di-title[class],
.tdi-card .tdi-brieftitle[class][class] {
  background: none !important;
  -webkit-background-clip: border-box !important;
  background-clip: border-box !important;
  -webkit-text-fill-color: currentColor !important;
  color: #14213d !important;
  font-family: var(--font-display) !important;
  font-weight: 700 !important;
  letter-spacing: -0.024em !important;
  line-height: 1.06 !important;
  width: auto !important;
  text-wrap: balance;
}
/* revamp1211: ONE weight, ONE colour. The two-tone — first as a gradient
   (1207), then as 700-against-300 (1209) — kept reading as an inconsistency
   rather than a device: the eye sees two different treatments in one phrase and
   asks what broke. The spans stay in the markup (harmless, and they keep the
   line breakable at the right point), but both halves are now the same weight
   and colour. Prominence comes from size, weight and tracking alone, which is
   what "normal but prominent" actually means. */
.di-title-name,
.di-title-kind { font-weight: 700; color: #14213d; letter-spacing: -0.024em; }

/* Room to breathe. The pill, the title and the date line were on a flat 10px
   rhythm, which is why the lockup read as cramped — a masthead needs its title
   set apart from the label above it and the metadata below. */
.di-page .di-mast.di-mast--v2[class][class][class] { gap: 14px !important; margin: 0 0 14px !important; }
.di-mast--v2 .di-title[class] { margin: 2px 0 0 !important; }
.tdi-mast2 { gap: 12px !important; }
.tdi-card .tdi-brieftitle[class][class] { margin: 1px 0 0 !important; }

/* ═══ revamp1209 — the colour is the ICON's, not the text's ═════════════════

   An open accordion tinted its parent name and every subtopic link with the
   topic hue, so the same list changed colour as you opened it and the copy
   read as links rather than text (#img1400/1401). The scheme belongs to the
   icon; the words stay the navy they are when the card is closed — which is
   also the only value the eye can compare across fourteen different hues. */
#content .aiidd-tree .aiidd-parent[data-open="true"] .aiidd-parent-name[class],
#content .aiidd-tree .aiidd-parent[open] .aiidd-parent-name[class],
/* The subtopic links are `.aiidd-vlink` / `.aiidd-vall` inside
   `.aiidd-parent-body > .aiidd-vlist`, and 1207 tints them with
   `color-mix(var(--tc) 64%, #14213d)` at `.aiidd-parent[style*="--tc"]
   .aiidd-vall[class]` — one attribute selector deeper than a plain class, so
   this has to name them the same way. */
#content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[style*="--tc"] .aiidd-vlink[class],
#content .aiidd-tree > .aiidd-parent[style*="--tc"] .aiidd-vall[class],
#content .aiidd-tree .aiidd-parent .aiidd-parent-body a,
#content .dih .dih-group[data-open="true"] .dih-grouptitle[class],
#content .ph-body .pdir .pdir-card .pdir-sub[class] {
  color: #1e2d44 !important;
}

/* ═══ revamp1209 — the sidebar joins the scheme ═════════════════════════════
   Its topic rows now carry --tc (set in renderStickyHeroBar), so the parent's
   glyph gets the filled chip every other directory gives it and the subtopics
   under it inherit the hue as bare glyphs. Names are untouched: colour-coding
   the text as well was the overkill the accordions just had removed. */
.navmenu-topic-acc .navmenu-topic-icon,
.navmenu-topic-link .navmenu-topic-icon {
  background: var(--tc, #eef2fa) !important;
  color: #fff !important;
  border-radius: 9px;
  width: 26px; height: 26px;
  display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
  flex: none;
}
.navmenu-topic-acc .navmenu-topic-icon svg,
.navmenu-topic-link .navmenu-topic-icon svg { width: 15px; height: 15px; }
/* Subtopic rows: the hue as ink only — no chip, matching the Topics page. */
.navmenu-topic-acc .navmenu-subtopics .navmenu-subtopic-link svg { color: var(--tc, currentColor); }
.navmenu-topic-acc[open] .navmenu-topic-summary { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tc, #2f4d9e) 7%, transparent); }

/* revamp1210 — the sidebar's expanded subtopics get their glyphs, matching the
   Topics-page accordions: bare marks in the parent's hue, no chip. The chip is
   what marks a PARENT; giving one to every subtopic would flatten that
   distinction and crowd a 288px rail. */
.navmenu-subtopic-link {
  display: flex !important;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 9px;
}
.navmenu-subtopic-ic {
  flex: none;
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  width: 17px; height: 17px;
  color: var(--tc, #6b7a96);
}
.navmenu-subtopic-ic svg { width: 15px; height: 15px; display: block; }
/* The parent row ("All World") keeps its trailing arrow pushed to the end. */
.navmenu-subtopic-parent .navmenu-subtopic-arrow { margin-left: auto; }

/* revamp1210b — the homepage hero chips are the same control as a topic page's
   related-topic pills, so they carry the same mark: the topic's glyph inside
   the pill, inked in its hue, no chip behind it. Mirrors .tbh-sub-ic exactly —
   including the -2px pull, which keeps the glyph optically inside the pill's
   radius rather than sitting on it. */
.home-hero-chip { gap: 7px; }
.home-hero-chip-ic {
  display: inline-grid; place-items: center; flex: none;
  width: 16px; height: 16px; margin-left: -2px;
  color: var(--tc, #4a5f8f);
}
.home-hero-chip-ic svg { display: block; width: 15px; height: 15px; }
@media (hover: hover) {
  .home-hero-chip:hover .home-hero-chip-ic { color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tc, #1d4ed8) 82%, #000); }
}

/* revamp1211 — the card's masthead stands down when the briefing opens.
   The expanded view renders its OWN masthead (topic pill, title, full date, the
   ✕), so with the card's still showing you got the title, then the title again
   four lines later. The old hide rules name `.tdi-eyebrow` / `.tdi-metarow`,
   the classes this markup replaced in 1207 — so nothing was hiding the new one. */
.tdi-card--hero2.is-open .tdi-mast2,
.tdi-card.is-open .tdi-mast2 { display: none !important; }
